::: {.doc-subject}

Get a tabular report of the comparison.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/pointblank/blob/main/pointblank/_compare.py#L2439-L2540){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Comparison.get_tabular_report(
    limit=10,
    title=None,
    lang=None,
    locale=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

The report brings everything about a comparison together in one table, for reading
rather than for further processing. It's what's shown when a comparison is displayed in a
notebook, and calling `get_tabular_report()` directly lets the report be customized and
saved, for example to attach to a pull request or to share with whoever owns the data.

The report has a header with the verdict, the row status counts, and the comparison
settings, followed by sections (as row groups) for schema differences, per-column
mismatches, and samples of changed, missing, extra, and duplicate-key rows. Changed rows
show only the columns that differ, with the source and target values (and the difference,
for numbers). Sections with nothing to show are omitted.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[limit]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[10]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The maximum number of sample rows shown per row status.

<code>[title]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   An optional title replacing the default.

<code>[lang]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The language of the report's labels, as a two-letter language code (e.g., `"fr"`,
    `"de"`) or one of the other Pointblank reporting languages. English by default.
    Values from the tables (keys, column names, and data) are shown as they are.

<code>[locale]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The locale used to format counts and percentages (e.g., the thousands separator).
    By default, it's the same as `lang=`.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [GT]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A Great Tables object.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}



Let's compare two months of a payroll table. Naming the tables with `source_name=` and
`target_name=` makes the report easier to read.

::: {#23328169 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import pointblank as pb
import polars as pl

payroll_jan = pl.DataFrame(
    {
        "emp_id": [101, 102, 103, 104],
        "dept": ["Sales", "Sales", "Ops", "Ops"],
        "salary": [52000, 61000, 58000, 47000],
    }
)
payroll_feb = pl.DataFrame(
    {
        "emp_id": [101, 102, 103, 105],
        "dept": ["Sales", "Sales", "Ops", "Ops"],
        "salary": [52000, 63500, 58000, 49000],
    }
)

comparison = pb.compare(
    payroll_jan,
    payroll_feb,
    keys="emp_id",
    source_name="payroll_jan",
    target_name="payroll_feb",
)

comparison.get_tabular_report()
```

